0,6,6],[6,6,0,6,6,0,6,6,0,6,6,0,6,6],[6,0,0,6,6,0,6,0,0,6,6,0,6,6],[6,6,0,6,6,0,6,6,0,6,6,0,6,6],[6,0,0,6,6,0,6,6,0,6,0,0,6,6],[6,6,0,6,6,0,6,6,0,6,6,0,6,6]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The columns are the key: the grid is a fixed lattice of 5 \"slot columns\" (x = 1, 4, 7, 10, 13). Each slot column has holes (0) in some of its odd rows. Between the slot columns are separators: every x ≡ 2 (mod 3) is entirely 0.\n\n**What the output does.** I compared the slot columns of pair 2 directly. Read as hole bits from top to bottom:\n\n| input slot | bits | holes |\n|---|---|---|\n| x=1 | 010110 | 3 |\n| x=4 | 111011 | 5 |\n| x=7 | 010000 | 1 |\n| x=10 | 110011 | 4 |\n| x=13 | 000101 | 2 |\n\nThe output's slot columns, left to right, are exactly the input's x=4, x=10, x=1, x=13, x=7. Each whole column pattern moves intact. The order is by hole count descending: 5, 4, 3, 2, 1. The fact sheet points the same way: the output's 0-objects at x=1, 4, 7, 10 have strictly growing sizes (19, 18, 17, ...). A bigger 0-object means more holes, and those columns now sit on the left.\n\nSo the rule is **sort the slot columns so the one with the most holes is on the left**. I use a stable sort so that ties keep their original order. The program checks all four pairs; if a tie mattered and the stable order were wrong, the verifier would show it.\n\n**Test input.** Its slot columns have these hole counts:\n- x=1: 2\n- x=4: 3\n- x=7: 1\n- x=10: 5\n- x=13: 3\n\nThe output order is therefore x=10, x=4, x=13, x=1, x=7. Here x=4 and x=13 tie, so they keep their left-to-right order.\n\n**How the program finds the slots.** It does not hard-code the slots. A slot column is any column that is neither all background nor all 0. The background is the most common colour. The program pulls each slot column out, sorts them by number of 0 cells (descending, stable), and writes them back into the same slot positions one at a time. It emits a teaching stage after each column is placed.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    slots = [x for x in range(W)\n             if not np.all(g[:, x] == 0) and not np.all(g[:, x] == bg)]\n    cols = [g[:, x].copy() for x in slots]\n    counts = [int(np.sum(c == 0)) for c in cols]\n    order = sorted(range(len(cols)), key=lambda i: -counts[i])  # stable\n    out = g.copy()\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Identify slot columns %s with hole counts %s; sort them by hole count, most holes first.\" % (slots, counts))\n    for pos, i in enumerate(order):\n        out[:, slots[pos]] = cols[i]\n        emit(out.copy(), \"Place the column with %d holes (from x=%d) into slot x=%d.\" % (counts[i], slots[i], slots[pos]))\n    emit(out.copy(), \"All slot columns reordered by descending hole count.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The background is the most common colour."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each slot column has holes (0) in some of its odd rows."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Read as hole bits from top to bottom:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"| input slot | bits | holes |"}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The order is by hole count descending: 5, 4, 3, 2, 1."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Test input.** Its slot columns have these hole counts:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The program pulls each slot column out, sorts them by number of 0 cells (descending, stable), and writes them back into the same slot positions one at a time."}],"order":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The order is by hole count descending: 5, 4, 3, 2, 1."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the rule is **sort the slot columns so the one with the most holes is on the left**."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"I use a stable sort so that ties keep their original order."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"52b08050a29aaa65d084b8652d56864079b5f9347d804a34a85469392edba52b","task_sha256":"1499daf1621e2723d875bd5b1e259c476cb1b197ad187f83d270ef7e4a2c2fd7","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
